          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a standard 40- to 50-gallon tank, Angi and HomeAdvisor put typical installed costs at roughly $900 to $1,850, with averages around $1,350. Tankless installs commonly run $1,400 to $3,900 or more, and heat pump units vary widely depending on electrical and space needs. Code upgrades, permits and regional labor rates explain most of the spread between quotes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it cheaper to replace a gas or electric water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "An electric tank is usually the cheaper swap, because the heater costs less and the installer only has to reconnect wiring and water lines. Gas tanks cost more to buy and require gas and vent work. Electric heaters often cost more to run in most regions, though, so the cheaper installation is not always the cheaper choice over the life of the heater."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a water heater replacement take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A straightforward like-for-like tank swap typically takes a plumber two to four hours, including draining the old tank, setting the new one, reconnecting and testing. Add time for corroded valves, vent repairs, attic installations or code upgrades. Switching to tankless or heat pump is often a full-day job or longer because of venting, gas line or electrical work."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a permit to replace a water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In most places, yes, especially for gas heaters. The permit triggers an inspection of the gas connection, venting, relief valve discharge and other safety items. Requirements and fees vary by city and county, so check with your local building department or ask your plumber, who usually pulls the permit as part of the job."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I repair or replace my water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Repair cheap failures such as elements, thermostats, thermocouples and igniters, especially on a heater under eight years old. Replace when the tank itself leaks, when the water stays rusty after flushing, or when an expensive repair is needed on a heater past roughly two thirds of its expected life. Check the warranty before deciding on any major part."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does homeowners insurance cover water heater replacement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Standard policies generally do not pay to replace a heater that wore out, because age and corrosion are maintenance issues. Many policies do cover sudden water damage a failing heater causes to the home, subject to your deductible and terms. Read your policy or ask your agent, and keep permits and invoices, since an insurer may ask for them after a claim."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a more expensive water heater worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sometimes. A longer tank warranty usually signals a heavier build, a better anode or more insulation, and it can be worth the premium if you plan to stay in the home. Paying extra for features your household will not use, or for oversized capacity, rarely pays off. Match the heater to your peak hot water demand rather than buying the biggest model offered."
          }
