    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A like-for-like tank swap usually lands somewhere around $900 to $1,850 installed, according to the cost ranges Angi and HomeAdvisor publish, while tankless and heat pump conversions commonly run $1,400 to $3,900 or more. The spread inside those ranges is not the heater. It is labor, the permit, and whatever the inspector says your old installation was getting away with. Know those line items before you call, and you can tell a fair quote from a padded one in about two minutes.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="cost-by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Replacement Costs, by Heater Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the national ranges, because they tell you whether a quote is in the normal band before you look at any detail. Angi puts a typical tank-style replacement at roughly $881 to $1,826 installed, with an average near $1,346, and HomeAdvisor's range for the same job is almost identical. Tankless installs sit higher, at roughly $1,400 to $3,900 in both sources, and HomeAdvisor lists hybrid heat pump water heaters at about $1,200 to $3,500. Other cost guides put heat pump installs higher still once electrical and condensate work are included.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those ranges are wide because they mix very different jobs. A 40-gallon electric tank going back into the same closet, on the same circuit, with the same pipes, is the cheapest water heater job there is. A 50-gallon gas tank that needs a new vent connector and an expansion tank is a step up. A gas tank swapped for a condensing tankless unit with new venting through the wall and an upsized gas line is a different project entirely, and it is the reason the top of the tankless range exists.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within the tank category, gas usually costs more than electric for the same capacity. The heater itself is pricier, and the installer has to disconnect and reconnect a gas line, check the venting, and test for leaks and proper draft. Electric tanks are simpler to swap, but they cost more to run in most of the country, which is worth weighing if you are choosing rather than replacing like for like. Our <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">guide to the types of water heaters</a> lays out the running-cost side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity moves the price less than people expect. Going from 40 to 50 gallons adds a modest amount to the heater cost. The bigger jump comes above 55 gallons, because federal efficiency standards since 2015 push large tanks toward heat pump or condensing designs, which are pricier and physically bigger. If your current tank is 50 gallons and you are thinking of upsizing, check that the new unit actually fits through the door and into the space before the quote is written around it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heater type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical installed range</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where the money goes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas tank (40-50 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $900-$1,850</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unit, gas and vent reconnection, draft test</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Electric tank (40-50 gal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low end of the tank range</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unit, wiring reconnection, simplest swap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tankless (gas)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1,400-$3,900+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Venting, gas line sizing, mounting, condensate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heat pump (hybrid)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1,200-$3,500+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unit cost, condensate drain, space and airflow</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat those numbers as a sanity check, not a price list. Regional labor rates swing totals by hundreds of dollars, and a quote well above the range is not automatically a rip-off if your installation needs real work. What matters is that every dollar above a basic swap is tied to a specific line item you can ask about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="labor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Labor: The Biggest Variable on the Quote
          </h2>

          <p>
            HomeAdvisor estimates that labor makes up around half of a typical water heater project budget, and that matches how quotes are usually built. A straightforward tank swap is often a two-to-four-hour job for an experienced plumber. Draining and disconnecting the old heater, moving it out, setting the new one, making up the water, gas or electrical connections, filling, purging air and testing takes most of a morning even when nothing goes wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            Things go wrong in predictable places. Corroded shutoff valves that will not close fully, galvanized nipples that snap, a gas flex connector that is too old to reuse, a vent pipe that falls apart when touched, or an attic installation that means carrying a 120-pound tank up a ladder all add time. A good installer will look at your current setup before quoting and price those risks in, which is why a quote over the phone without a visit or photos tends to grow on installation day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most plumbers quote water heater replacement as a flat price rather than by the hour, which protects you from a slow job but also means the price already contains a cushion for surprises. That is fine, as long as the flat price says what is included. You want to see the heater model, the connectors and valves being replaced, the permit, haul-away, and any code items listed out. A single number with no detail makes it impossible to compare two bids.
          </p>

          <p>
            Big-box stores sell installation packages that look cheaper because the base price covers only a basic swap. The add-ons, such as new valves, an expansion tank, venting parts, a drip pan or permit handling, are priced separately. Some homeowners do well with these programs; others find the final bill matches a local plumber's. Compare on the completed total, not the headline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Doing it yourself saves most of the labor, and an electric tank swap is within reach for a confident DIYer. Gas is a different matter. Gas connections, venting and combustion air are where mistakes become carbon monoxide or fire risks, and many jurisdictions require a licensed installer or at least a permit and inspection for any gas appliance. If you are not certain your work would pass an inspection, the labor line is money well spent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="permits-haul-away" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Permits, Inspection and Haul-Away
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most municipalities require a permit to replace a water heater, even like for like, because the job touches gas, venting, pressure relief and sometimes electrical work. Permit fees vary a lot by city and county, from tens of dollars in some places to a few hundred in others. A licensed plumber normally pulls the permit and adds the fee to the quote, sometimes with a small charge for the trip to meet the inspector.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is tempting to skip the permit and save the fee. The problem shows up later. An unpermitted gas appliance can complicate an insurance claim if the heater leaks or causes a fire, and it can surface during a home sale inspection. The permit also buys you a second set of eyes on the work: the inspector checks the venting, the gas connection, the temperature and pressure relief discharge line, and the seismic strapping where required. That check is the main reason the permit exists.
          </p>

          <p>
            Haul-away of the old tank is often bundled into a plumber's quote, and when it is listed separately it is usually a modest charge. A 40- or 50-gallon steel tank is heavy and awkward, and many trash services will not take it curbside. If you are doing the job yourself, a local scrap metal yard will often accept an old tank, though you have to drain it and get it there. Some municipalities also run bulk pickup programs that accept appliances.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask whether the quote includes disposal and whether the permit fee is an estimate or the actual amount. Those two lines are small but they are the most common source of "we forgot to mention" charges on the final invoice, and a plumber who lists them up front is usually one who has thought through the rest of the job as well.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the quote does not mention a permit, ask why.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Some jurisdictions genuinely do not require one for an electric like-for-like swap. Many do for gas. A plumber should be able to tell you which applies where you live in one sentence.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="code-upgrades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Code Upgrades That Get Added to the Bill
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where quotes for the same heater diverge most. A water heater installed fifteen years ago was built to the code of fifteen years ago, and replacing it usually means bringing the installation up to the current code your jurisdiction has adopted. None of these items is optional when the inspector requires it, and none of them is a scam just because your old heater worked without it.
          </p>

          <p>
            An expansion tank is the most common addition. If your home has a pressure-reducing valve, a backflow preventer or a check valve at the meter, the plumbing is a closed system and heated water has nowhere to expand, so the model plumbing codes require a means of controlling thermal expansion. The tank itself is inexpensive and installs on the cold supply line. We explain how to tell whether you need one and how to size it in our <a href="/water-heater-expansion-tank/">water heater expansion tank guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            A drip pan with a drain line is required where a leak would damage the building, which typically means attics, upper floors and finished spaces. The pan is cheap; the cost is in running a drain line to somewhere it can discharge. The temperature and pressure relief valve also needs a proper discharge pipe that runs to within a short distance of the floor or to an approved location, and older installations often have one that is the wrong material, too short, or missing entirely. Our <a href="/water-heater-pressure-relief-valve/">pressure relief valve guide</a> covers what a correct one looks like.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas heaters bring venting into play. If a furnace was replaced with a high-efficiency model that vents through the wall, the water heater may now be the only appliance using an oversized masonry chimney, which can cause poor draft and condensation. Fixing that can mean a chimney liner or a different vent route, and it is one of the larger surprises on a gas quote. New vent connectors, a sediment trap on the gas line, and seismic straps in states such as California are other common items.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical upgrades show up less often on straight tank swaps but do appear: a missing disconnect, undersized wire, or a breaker that does not match the new heater's rating. If you are moving to a heat pump water heater, the installer also needs a place for the condensate to drain. Ask the plumber to separate code items from optional ones on the quote so you can see exactly what the inspector requires.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switching-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Switching Types Changes the Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing a tank with a tank is a known quantity. Switching to tankless or heat pump is where installed cost climbs, and where the long-term savings have to justify it. The heater is only part of the difference. A gas tankless unit usually needs a larger gas supply than a tank because it fires at a much higher input when hot water is running, and condensing models need new PVC venting and a condensate drain. Whole-house electric tankless units can require a substantial electrical service upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless water heater cost guide</a> breaks those conversion items down, and <a href="/replace-water-heater-with-tankless/">replacing a tank with tankless</a> walks through whether the switch makes sense for your house. The short version: tankless pays off best for households that run out of hot water with a tank, plan to stay in the home long enough to recover the extra install cost, and already have adequate gas supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pump water heaters are the efficiency leader for electric homes, and the installed cost premium over a standard electric tank can be partly offset by lower running costs and, in some areas, utility rebates. Note that the federal 25C tax credit, which covered 30% of a qualifying heat pump water heater up to $2,000, ended for equipment installed after December 31, 2025, so do not plan your budget around it. Check your utility and state energy office for current rebates instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pump units also need space and air. They pull heat from the surrounding room, so manufacturers specify a minimum air volume or louvered doors, and they need a condensate drain. Some newer models run on a standard 120-volt outlet, which can avoid a new circuit in a home that currently has a gas heater. Our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">best heat pump water heater guide</a> covers the models and their space requirements.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your tank failed and you need hot water tomorrow, a like-for-like replacement is the fast, predictable option. Switching types is a better decision when you plan it before the old heater dies, because it gives you time to get the gas line, venting or electrical work quoted properly instead of accepting whatever is on the truck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-vs-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Repair vs Replace: When a Fix Is Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every hot water problem needs a new heater. Many of the most common failures are cheap parts. On an electric tank, a failed heating element or thermostat is an inexpensive part and a short job, and it is the usual reason for lukewarm water or no hot water at all. Our <a href="/water-heater-element/">water heater element guide</a> covers testing and replacing one. On a gas tank, a thermocouple, flame sensor or igniter is similarly affordable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other repairs are worth doing because they extend the life of the tank. Replacing a depleted anode rod is one of the best-value jobs in home maintenance, because the anode is what stops the steel tank from rusting through. Our <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">anode rod replacement guide</a> explains when to check it. Flushing sediment out of the tank is another; we cover it in <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some failures end the conversation. A leak from the tank itself, as opposed to a fitting, valve or connection, cannot be repaired, because it means the steel inside has corroded through. Rusty hot water that persists after flushing, a tank sitting in water, or a bulge in the jacket all point to replacement. Our <a href="/water-heater-leaking/">water heater leaking guide</a> helps you tell a fixable leak from a failed tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everything in between, the usual rule of thumb is that if a repair costs more than about half of a replacement and the heater is past roughly two thirds of its expected life, replace it. The Department of Energy puts typical tank life at around 8 to 12 years, and our <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">guide to how long water heaters last</a> covers what shortens or extends it. If you are not sure how old yours is, the serial number usually encodes the manufacture date, as explained in <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            A gas control valve is the classic borderline case. It is one of the most expensive parts on a gas tank, and on an 11-year-old heater it rarely makes sense to fit one. On a 4-year-old heater still under parts warranty it often does, because the manufacturer covers the valve and you pay only labor. Always check the warranty before deciding either way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="getting-quotes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Get a Fair Quote
          </h2>

          <p>
            Get at least two quotes, and give every plumber the same information: the heater's fuel type, capacity, location in the house, and photos of the top of the tank, the vent, the gas or electrical connection, and the area around it. Photos let a plumber spot code issues before the visit, which turns a vague estimate into a firm number and cuts down the surprises on installation day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask each quote to list the heater make and model, the warranty length on the tank and on labor, whether the permit and haul-away are included, and every code item they plan to add. Then compare like with like. A cheaper quote with a 6-year tank warranty and no expansion tank is not cheaper than a quote with a 9- or 12-year tank warranty and a code-compliant install if the inspector would require the expansion tank anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of quotes that only offer the most expensive heater the plumber stocks, or that push a tankless conversion when you asked for a tank replacement. Upselling is not always bad advice, but the plumber should be able to explain why the upgrade suits your household, in numbers. If you want to research the heater itself first, our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heater guide</a> and <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands comparison</a> are good places to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing affects price. An emergency replacement on a weekend or holiday usually costs more, and you have less room to compare bids when you are standing in a wet basement. If your heater is approaching the end of its expected life, getting quotes before it fails is the easiest way to save money on this job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the paperwork. The invoice, permit sign-off, model and serial number, and warranty registration are what you will need if the heater fails early, and they also answer the first question a home buyer's inspector will ask. Registering the heater with the manufacturer takes five minutes and can be the difference between a covered tank and a denied claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing headline prices instead of totals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A low base price often excludes valves, connectors, permits, expansion tanks and haul-away. Two quotes are only comparable when both list the same items. Ask each plumber for a completed total covering everything needed to pass inspection, then compare those numbers side by side.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the permit to save the fee</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An unpermitted gas installation can complicate insurance claims and home sales, and it means nobody checked the venting or relief valve discharge. The fee is small relative to the job. Let the plumber pull it, and treat the inspection as free quality control on work you paid for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Repairing a tank that is actually leaking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If water is coming from the tank body rather than a fitting or valve, the steel has corroded through and no repair will hold. Spending money on parts for a failed tank only delays the inevitable and risks water damage. Confirm the source of the leak before approving any repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the old setup was code compliant</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many older installations lack expansion tanks, drip pans, correct relief discharge lines or proper venting. Those items get added at replacement because current code requires them, not because the plumber is padding the bill. Ask which items are code and which are optional upgrades.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting for the heater to fail before getting quotes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Emergency replacements leave no room to compare bids or plan a switch to tankless or heat pump. If your tank is past ten years old, collect quotes now. You will pay less, choose better, and avoid the water damage that often comes with a tank failure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to replace a water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a standard 40- to 50-gallon tank, Angi and HomeAdvisor put typical installed costs at roughly $900 to $1,850, with averages around $1,350. Tankless installs commonly run $1,400 to $3,900 or more, and heat pump units vary widely depending on electrical and space needs. Code upgrades, permits and regional labor rates explain most of the spread between quotes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to replace a gas or electric water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An electric tank is usually the cheaper swap, because the heater costs less and the installer only has to reconnect wiring and water lines. Gas tanks cost more to buy and require gas and vent work. Electric heaters often cost more to run in most regions, though, so the cheaper installation is not always the cheaper choice over the life of the heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a water heater replacement take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A straightforward like-for-like tank swap typically takes a plumber two to four hours, including draining the old tank, setting the new one, reconnecting and testing. Add time for corroded valves, vent repairs, attic installations or code upgrades. Switching to tankless or heat pump is often a full-day job or longer because of venting, gas line or electrical work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit to replace a water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most places, yes, especially for gas heaters. The permit triggers an inspection of the gas connection, venting, relief valve discharge and other safety items. Requirements and fees vary by city and county, so check with your local building department or ask your plumber, who usually pulls the permit as part of the job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I repair or replace my water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Repair cheap failures such as elements, thermostats, thermocouples and igniters, especially on a heater under eight years old. Replace when the tank itself leaks, when the water stays rusty after flushing, or when an expensive repair is needed on a heater past roughly two thirds of its expected life. Check the warranty before deciding on any major part.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does homeowners insurance cover water heater replacement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard policies generally do not pay to replace a heater that wore out, because age and corrosion are maintenance issues. Many policies do cover sudden water damage a failing heater causes to the home, subject to your deductible and terms. Read your policy or ask your agent, and keep permits and invoices, since an insurer may ask for them after a claim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a more expensive water heater worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. A longer tank warranty usually signals a heavier build, a better anode or more insulation, and it can be worth the premium if you plan to stay in the home. Paying extra for features your household will not use, or for oversized capacity, rarely pays off. Match the heater to your peak hot water demand rather than buying the biggest model offered.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

